# Mind maps — the markmap markdown dialect

`context/instructions/mindmap-markmap.md` is the contract for writing a mind map
as markdown in the markmap dialect, and for managing the resulting `.map.md`
document in the project's corpus. Read it together with
`context/instructions/mindmap.md` (the format-agnostic doctrine: the outline
first, one keyword per node, the budgets) and `context/instructions/wiki.md`
(the relation discipline for corpus documents). The doctrine file owns the
budgets; this file owns the dialect mechanics.

## The silent-drop list (read this first)

The parser decides what the document *is* by discarding what it does not
recognise. There is no error and no warning — only absence.

- **Plain paragraphs are dropped verbatim.**
- **Blockquotes are dropped.**
- Anything outside this selector is dropped: `h1..h6`, `ul`, `ol`, `li`, `table`,
  `pre` (fenced code), and a paragraph whose only child is an image.

So never put a sentence in a map where you expect it to appear as a node. The
failure mode is content disappearing silently, which is why this list comes
first: check it before trusting anything you wrote.

## Skeleton

- Exactly one root: a single `#` heading, or a frontmatter `title` as the root.
- `##` for main branches; deeper nesting through `###` headings and nested `-`
  lists.
- Headings and lists only — see the silent-drop list above.

## One child level per parent

Do not mix a loose top-level list with sub-headings under the same parent: a new
child of a smaller level resets the parent's children, so the discarded block
does not merge — it disappears. Pick one level per parent and stay in it.

## Frontmatter is the options channel, not a container

- Options go under the `markmap:` frontmatter key: `title`, `color`,
  `colorFreezeLevel`, `duration`, `maxWidth`, `initialExpandLevel`, `extraJs`,
  `extraCss`, `htmlParser`.
- Content never belongs in frontmatter.
- These options are honoured only by renderers that implement them; the plugins a
  consuming tool registers decide whether maths, syntax highlighting and
  checkboxes render at all.

## Inline formatting survives

Emphasis, `sup`/`sub`, links, images and `[ ]`/`[x]` checkboxes render inside a
node — subject to the plugin caveat above.

## Folding markers (with a cost)

`<!-- markmap: fold -->` and `<!-- markmap: foldAll -->` set the initial fold
state. The cost: they are not standard markdown, so any renderer that is not this
dialect shows the raw comment.

## Group tags (overlay)

`#group/<name>` inside a node's text marks it as a member of a cross-cutting
group. This is a display convention, not parser syntax: a rendering tool strips
the tag from the label and draws one convex-hull boundary around every member,
including members that are not consecutive siblings — which the XMindMark `[B]`
rounded rectangles cannot wrap.

- The tag is a token **inside** a node, not a standalone heading: a line that is
  exactly `#group/x` parses as a level-1 heading.
- A node may carry several tags: `topic #group/auth #group/core`.
- The name is `[A-Za-z0-9][\w-]*`. The tag is node-scoped and unrelated to the
  document's `tags:` frontmatter.
- A group of fewer than three members falls back to its bounding rectangle.

## Leaves

Tables, fenced code blocks and image-only paragraphs become leaf nodes. Use them
as leaves only; never hide prose inside one.

## Version scope

The element mapping above was read against the 0.18.x `markmap-html-parser`.
Re-verify it against the installed version before relying on a parser edge case,
because a version bump degrades silently: content drops rather than erroring.

## Document management (`.map.md`)

A map is an existing corpus document in a particular form, not a new kind:

- `.map.md` is a **suffix modifier on an existing document kind**, not a kind of
  its own.
- The document id is derived by stripping the `.md` extension only, so the map
  carries its **own** id (`context/notes/plan.map.md` → `context/notes/plan.map`)
  and links to its base document rather than aliasing it.
- Frontmatter and `kind` are inherited from the base document type; the map adds
  no kind-specific schema.
- **Typed relations** live in the `relations:` frontmatter map over the closed
  verb vocabulary: `supersedes`, `depends_on`, `refers_to`, `refines`,
  `implements`, `conflicts_with`, `part_of`, `contains`.
- `[[target|label]]` wikilinks, optionally with a `#anchor`, are ordinary
  navigation and are **not** graph edges on their own.
- **No duplication** — content lives in the map *or* in its base document, never
  in both. A map that copies its base creates a second source of truth that will
  drift: the map carries the shape, the base document keeps the prose.
